**Description**
Logging events is a fundamental function of software runtimes. It offers valuable insights into the system's behavior, helping to identify what occurred during execution and what might have gone wrong in the event of an incident. By analyzing logs, developers can better understand the scale and impact of issues, enabling more effective troubleshooting and optimization.

**Implementation**
For this specific applet we will be implementing a basic logger event using buttons, date and dynamic rendered list.
![image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/21645145-1977-478f-adae-f6f3b16b58c4)

The following code is for the user interface:
```
<div class="applet-3">
      <h1>Event Button Logger</h1>
      <h3 id="logCount">Total Logs: 0</h3>

      <br>
      <button class="btn btn-primary" id="logButton">Click Me</button>
      <button class="btn btn-secondary"  id="clearButton">Clear</button>

      <h2>Collected Data:</h2>
     <div id="cardContainer" style="width:20rem"></div>
</div>
```
The constructor on the   class:
```
constructor(buttonId, cardContainerId, clearButtonId, logCountId) {
        this.logButton = document.getElementById(buttonId);
        this.cardContainer = document.getElementById(cardContainerId);
        this.clearButton = document.getElementById(clearButtonId);
        this.logCountElement = document.getElementById(logCountId); 
        this.loggedData = [];

        this.logButton.addEventListener('click', () => this.logData());
        this.clearButton.addEventListener('click', () => this.clearLogs());
}
```

